WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.320
Hello and welcome to another episode
with ItsDrdan and this time we're going

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.850
to be looking at intermolecular forces.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.509
So when we're looking at intermolecular
forces the big thing here is that

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.269
we're trying to understand how
molecules can interact with each

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.055
other without creating actual bonds.

00:00:33.535 --> 00:00:38.965
So something like water, for example,
when you have a glass of water, all

00:00:38.965 --> 00:00:42.445
these individual molecules that are
in here, they're all water molecules.

00:00:42.445 --> 00:00:45.165
So H2O, but what are they all doing?

00:00:45.165 --> 00:00:47.294
Are they just kind of like all
sitting there, just all wandering

00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:49.394
around or are they interacting?

00:00:49.874 --> 00:00:52.214
And this is where
intermolecular forces comes in.

00:00:52.575 --> 00:00:57.035
So the whole idea behind this, the
word inter is referring to between

00:00:57.125 --> 00:01:04.145
molecules and it all feeds off of
the concept of Dipoles, uh, from the,

00:01:04.195 --> 00:01:06.615
from previous videos and polarity.

00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:11.705
So if I have two dipoles or
two similar molecules, they can

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:15.664
interact with each other like
magnets attracted to one another.

00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:18.945
So if you ever take two magnets,
very opposite charges, they

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.525
attract, they're going to bring
each other closer as a result.

00:01:23.155 --> 00:01:25.895
So these are attracting each other.

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:31.215
However, they are not bonding because
they are not sharing electrons.

00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:36.484
So going back to something like our
little water analogy, if I take a

00:01:36.494 --> 00:01:43.345
water molecule, so I have a Lewis
structure for water, and I label that

00:01:43.345 --> 00:01:48.175
with my delta pluses, delta negatives,
like so, what we're actually going to

00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:53.024
see is that Each of these different
positives and negatives can create

00:01:53.054 --> 00:01:59.945
an intermolecular force with another
molecule that is of similar quality.

00:02:00.294 --> 00:02:04.555
So let's say if I have
another water that is nearby.

00:02:05.644 --> 00:02:09.364
And for this one, we
also have the same ideas.

00:02:09.365 --> 00:02:13.174
We have the positive and
negative charges here.

00:02:13.614 --> 00:02:17.824
Well, I have negative,
positive, negative, positive.

00:02:18.184 --> 00:02:19.704
So we have...

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:25.970
An intermolecular force, or
IMF, so we have attraction.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:31.720
So whenever we have that attraction,
well, that is what we're looking for.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:33.380
So what types can we have?

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.230
Let's look at how we can
really tell the difference.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.429
So there's four major types.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:43.340
So they are called dispersion,
Dipole dipole, hydrogen

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.620
bonding, and ion dipole forces.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:53.289
So one thing you might see that there is
a lot of in this one is the word dipole.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:58.789
So you see it once, twice, three
times, um, and so what that's kind of

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.780
telling you is that Oh, so two out of
the four have the word dipole in it.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.810
That must be a major
characteristic of these.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.010
So they're all about the polarity that
we've talked about in previous videos.

00:03:10.279 --> 00:03:12.930
So if you weren't sure about
electronegativity and polarity, go back

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.710
to my last two videos, my other two videos
that were posted previously on them.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.380
I have two parts so far on that.

00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:21.995
Hopefully it will help kind of clear
that up before we talk about how

00:03:22.025 --> 00:03:23.505
they're attracted to one another.

00:03:24.105 --> 00:03:26.745
So let's go through them,
and how let's label them.

00:03:26.765 --> 00:03:29.205
So the first one, so dispersion.

00:03:29.594 --> 00:03:34.855
Dispersion is a force that
occurs between all molecules.

00:03:35.275 --> 00:03:43.160
Okay, so dispersion forces
occur between all molecules.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.079
So it's anything with electrons.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.970
Now, even though it's all
molecules, it's the strongest

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.010
force for non polar molecules.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.144
All right, so whenever we have a non
polar molecule, that's all it's capable

00:04:02.154 --> 00:04:05.464
of doing so that's whenever you see
something nonpolar and you could label it.

00:04:05.904 --> 00:04:08.454
You know, it's only force
is this so what's happening?

00:04:08.454 --> 00:04:12.954
So let's say if I take one of the
simplest nonpolar Elements out there

00:04:12.954 --> 00:04:14.515
and that would be like a noble gas.

00:04:14.524 --> 00:04:18.245
So what if I take a helium now?

00:04:18.264 --> 00:04:20.284
How many electrons does
a helium have around it?

00:04:20.284 --> 00:04:24.810
Well, if you had remember it goes hydrogen
then helium so it has two electrons.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:32.740
So if I have those two electrons that
are around it, how exactly is that going

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.520
to be kind of displayed around its core?

00:04:35.529 --> 00:04:40.870
Well, probability dictates it
should be in as far away from

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:41.910
each other as possible, right?

00:04:41.939 --> 00:04:43.379
Like charges repel.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:49.750
However, what if We have a helium
that collides with another helium.

00:04:50.159 --> 00:04:53.710
Being that helium is a gas,
collisions happen all the time.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:59.819
Um, how exactly would that
occur if they collide?

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:04.369
Well, if that were to happen,
that picture is going...

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.960
to change.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.159
So whenever we have a collision,
or it's the same way as if you were

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.650
kind of running and someone cut in
front of you and you had to stop, or

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.149
let's say if you had to slam on the
brakes of your car, what's to happen?

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.849
Well, you jut forward in that seatbelt
and you feel that momentum shift.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.960
So being that electrons are
really, really light, they're

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.080
going to fly forward as a result.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:36.940
So we have a concentration of
electrons on one side of the atom.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.210
Same thing with the other one, right?

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:43.430
Because they collided with each other, all
of a sudden, those electrons in the first

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.500
one repelled the ones in the other one.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.709
So when that happened, it caused
a temporary dipole to form.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.290
So we have a positive section,
negative, positive, negative.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:02.835
So we have temporary Instantaneous dipole.

00:06:03.765 --> 00:06:05.215
Instantaneous.

00:06:05.684 --> 00:06:06.484
Hopefully I spelled that right.

00:06:09.615 --> 00:06:15.495
Okay, so when that happens we have a
brief moment of attraction between these

00:06:15.904 --> 00:06:20.810
and it's an extremely weak Interaction.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.640
Okay, so that's the first one.

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:25.820
It's with nonpolar.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.070
Now, what are the trends
with it in terms of strength?

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:35.059
So this is with all molecules and but for
nonpolar, this is the strongest so the

00:06:35.059 --> 00:06:46.840
way that it grows in strength strength
increases as molar mass Increases so the

00:06:46.849 --> 00:06:53.320
bigger the molecule the more electrons
the stronger the dispersion forces.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.960
So that's stronger dispersion.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.060
That is our first force.

00:06:59.469 --> 00:07:00.630
Let's go into our second one.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.800
Our second one was dipole dipole.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.899
Alright, so what was
something with a dipole?

00:07:05.909 --> 00:07:11.189
Well, that was whenever we had a
molecule that was, that had a partial

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:14.410
positive and a partial negative charge.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.370
So let's draw a molecule
that would show that.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:22.409
So what if I had a C double
bonded to an O with two hydrogens?

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:23.940
on both sides.

