00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:33.900
But if you remember from previously,
one of our trends was that a CO bond is

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:40.469
polar, meaning that the oxygen on this
one is going to be slightly negative.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:43.280
So we'll have a delta
negative on this side.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:50.940
Delta plus on that now if this isn't
a similar solution of let's say other

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:59.669
formaldehyde So other, uh, CH2O So how
would they be attracted to each other?

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:04.704
Well, very similar to dispersion being
that these have permanent dive holes

00:08:05.335 --> 00:08:11.445
We can kind of maybe imagine that these
are all going to be about how they

00:08:11.445 --> 00:08:13.585
can kind of rotate in on themselves.

00:08:13.585 --> 00:08:17.585
So I take this molecule, and I rotate it.

00:08:17.965 --> 00:08:21.295
So that way the positive side is
exposed, maybe the negative side.

00:08:22.105 --> 00:08:25.334
And we bring it closer to,
let's say, another CH2.

00:08:28.745 --> 00:08:31.275
Well, we're going to see
that we have a delta plus.

00:08:32.855 --> 00:08:36.345
Sorry, delta negative, delta plus.

00:08:38.505 --> 00:08:41.344
And we can re label
the other side as well.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.400
So we have negative plus negative
plus, so they are going to make an

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:50.470
intermolecular force between each other.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:59.759
So this is our dipole dipole, and
this is between polar molecules.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:06.039
So it has to be polar as a result.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.470
So keep that in mind if we have
something that's polar, then we know

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.930
that it is going to be attracted.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:16.800
So that is dipole dipole.

00:09:17.889 --> 00:09:21.829
The next type of force is
whenever we have hydrogen bonding.

00:09:21.829 --> 00:09:25.550
So it has to be another
very specific definition.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:32.620
For this one, hydrogen bonding
is a subtype of dipole dipole.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:38.939
Hydrogen bonding has to fit the
definition where it has to look like this.

00:09:38.949 --> 00:09:44.790
We have to have Let's say an
atom X connected to a hydrogen.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.519
So that's where this part comes into play.

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:53.479
And that thing is going to be interacting.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:10:01.640
So that is where the bonding part
comes in with something that is.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:03.410
Electronegative.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:12.640
Okay, so in this instance X has to be
a very specific Electronegative atom it

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.910
has to be something that's a very very
negative So if you remember from our

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:20.340
electronegativity trends the farther
you were up into the right of the

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:25.500
periodic table The more electronegative
something was so something like fluorine

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:33.359
nitrogen Oxygen have to be one of our
atoms, so it can only be, hydrogen

00:10:33.359 --> 00:10:39.240
can only be attached to fluorine,
nitrogen, or oxygen, nothing else.

00:10:39.469 --> 00:10:44.809
So when we drew water earlier,
hydrogen bonding, we just didn't

00:10:44.809 --> 00:10:52.719
give it a name, so water, hydrogen
bonding to another water molecule.

00:10:53.814 --> 00:10:57.435
We showed an instance of hydrogen bonding.

00:10:58.335 --> 00:11:03.645
So, oxygen interacts with the hydrogen,
and we have something like this.

00:11:03.675 --> 00:11:05.474
And we can label the
different charges, right?

00:11:05.474 --> 00:11:13.665
We have minus, plus, minus, plus,
but this shows hydrogen bonding,

00:11:13.705 --> 00:11:17.344
because this fits our definition.

00:11:18.134 --> 00:11:24.075
Our definition in this instance is
an oxygen is bound to a hydrogen,

00:11:24.975 --> 00:11:29.425
And that's interacting with
another oxygen in this situation.

00:11:30.645 --> 00:11:34.115
So another example of hydrogen
bonding would be like if I take,

00:11:34.195 --> 00:11:41.425
okay, so if it has to be fluorine
or in hydrogen, what if I do HF and

00:11:41.425 --> 00:11:43.945
that was interacting with another HF?

00:11:44.735 --> 00:11:47.579
So in this case we have
delta plus, delta minus.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:52.810
That's going to be
interacting with another HF

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:00.730
and we can label them as positive
and minus and we can also show

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.080
an instance of hydrogen bonding.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:06.459
Now, with hydrogen bonding,
there's a little more nomenclature

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.109
or naming that comes into this.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:14.240
So there's the hydrogen bond donor and
there's also the hydrogen bond acceptor.

00:12:15.135 --> 00:12:22.584
The donor, so the hydrogen bond
donor, contains the hydrogen.

00:12:25.415 --> 00:12:32.444
So in this case, the hydrogen
bond donor is this molecule here

00:12:32.444 --> 00:12:34.244
because the hydrogen is interacting.

00:12:35.074 --> 00:12:40.325
Whereas on the left over here,
